1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,680
If you are opening Propeller for the first
time,

2
00:00:03,160 --> 00:00:06,850
here is the full player-prop research
workflow in the order that

3
00:00:06,860 --> 00:00:10,760
matters. Start with the exact question in
front of you:

4
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:14,080
the player, stat, direction, and current
line.

5
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:16,140
Those details stay together.

6
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:19,420
A strong opinion about a player is not
enough;

7
00:00:19,940 --> 00:00:23,070
the research only makes sense against the
number you can actually

8
00:00:23,080 --> 00:00:27,910
use. On desktop, choose a listed prop and
open its detail

9
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:30,640
view. First, find the line.

10
00:00:31,220 --> 00:00:34,480
Then confirm whether the analysis is
leaning More or Less.

11
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:37,870
On mobile, the same essentials appear at
the top of the

12
00:00:37,880 --> 00:00:41,460
prop detail screen, so you can verify the
player,

13
00:00:41,540 --> 00:00:44,220
market, and number before you read
anything else.

14
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:47,120
Next, read confidence correctly.

15
00:00:47,940 --> 00:00:52,130
Propeller's confidence score summarizes
the strength of the available directional

16
00:00:52,140 --> 00:00:53,810
evidence for the side shown.

17
00:00:53,820 --> 00:00:57,350
It is not a promise, and it is not the
player's

18
00:00:57,360 --> 00:00:59,360
exact chance of hitting the prop.

19
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:04,110
Use it to decide which analysis may be
worth opening first—not

20
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:06,000
as a reason to skip the research.

21
00:01:06,820 --> 00:01:09,180
Now move to the reason behind the score.

22
00:01:09,860 --> 00:01:13,450
The agent breakdown lets you see which
available signals support the

23
00:01:13,460 --> 00:01:17,170
lean, which signals conflict, and which
one is doing the most

24
00:01:17,180 --> 00:01:19,860
work. Depending on the sport and market,

25
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:23,980
that may include opportunity, matchup,
injury context,

26
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:28,420
pace or environment, recent performance,
and market information.

27
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:31,420
Do not expect every agent to agree.

28
00:01:31,980 --> 00:01:36,110
A useful analysis can include a strong
opportunity signal and a

29
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:38,120
caution from the matchup or market.

30
00:01:38,760 --> 00:01:40,720
The point is to understand the evidence,

31
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:43,190
not to turn every card into a vote.

32
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:47,930
On mobile, open Model Read and Agent
Breakdown for the same

33
00:01:47,940 --> 00:01:50,260
explanation in a more compact view.

34
00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:54,100
As you read the cards, ask three simple
questions.

35
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:57,300
What is the strongest reason for the
displayed side?

36
00:01:57,720 --> 00:02:00,550
Is that reason current, or is it based on
an older

37
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:04,410
trend? And is there a counterargument that
changes how much weight

38
00:02:04,420 --> 00:02:05,170
you give the lean?

39
00:02:05,180 --> 00:02:10,530
A high score with one important unresolved
concern deserves more review

40
00:02:10,540 --> 00:02:13,320
than a clean, broadly supported analysis.

41
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:17,930
The breakdown makes that judgment visible
instead of hiding it behind

42
00:02:17,940 --> 00:02:22,560
one number. Recent form belongs in that
same context.

43
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:26,410
A player who cleared a number in several
recent games may

44
00:02:26,420 --> 00:02:29,270
have had a different role, opponent, or
line.

45
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:32,400
Use the recent results to form a question,

46
00:02:32,820 --> 00:02:36,040
then use the agents and current
information to test it.

47
00:02:36,480 --> 00:02:39,940
Do not treat a hit-rate streak as a
forecast by itself.

48
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:42,960
Then check the context that can change.

49
00:02:43,620 --> 00:02:47,060
Confirm the displayed line is still the
line you are considering.

50
00:02:47,760 --> 00:02:49,970
Look for late lineup or injury news,

51
00:02:49,980 --> 00:02:53,170
and make sure you are not treating a
changed number as

52
00:02:53,180 --> 00:02:54,510
the same research question.

53
00:02:54,520 --> 00:02:58,240
If the evidence is missing, mixed, or
stale,

54
00:02:58,780 --> 00:03:00,600
that is a valid reason to pass.

55
00:03:01,560 --> 00:03:03,120
Passing is part of the workflow.

56
00:03:03,900 --> 00:03:06,470
You do not need to force a conclusion
because a prop

57
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:10,280
is available or because the confidence
score caught your eye.

58
00:03:10,840 --> 00:03:14,240
If the line has moved, a key player is
questionable,

59
00:03:14,760 --> 00:03:18,330
or the available agents point in different
directions without a clear

60
00:03:18,340 --> 00:03:22,120
explanation, wait for better information
or move on.

61
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:27,160
Here is the workflow to repeat: confirm
the exact line,

62
00:03:27,380 --> 00:03:30,500
read the direction and confidence, inspect
the agents,

63
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:35,470
verify freshness and context, then decide
whether the evidence is strong

64
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:37,020
enough to research further.

65
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:41,880
The goal is a clearer process—not an
opinion on every prop.

66
00:03:42,540 --> 00:03:45,110
You can explore the current Prop Analyzer
at PropellerPicks.com.

67
00:03:45,120 --> 00:03:49,960
Propeller Picks is a research and
analytics platform,

68
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:54,120
not a sportsbook. Player props involve
uncertainty,

69
00:03:54,520 --> 00:03:57,960
and nothing here is a guarantee or
financial advice.
